<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#undeutsche">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Undeutsche</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Undeutsche (&quot;non-Germans&quot;) was the term used in medieval and early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> for the native Estonian and Latvian population, as opposed to the German-speaking nobility, clergy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>. In towns such as Reval and Riga, Undeutsche worked as porters, carters, servants and labourers and were largely excluded from the merchant and craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and from town government.</p></div>
